  "sourceNotes": "Sugarplums are documented in English cookery from the late medieval period onward. The term originally referred to any small confection coated in layers of sugar, including aniseeds and caraway seeds; by the 18th and 19th centuries it was also applied to mixtures of dried fruits and spices shaped into rounds and rolled in sugar. The Victorian Christmas association comes partly from 'A Visit from St Nicholas' (Clement Clarke Moore, 1823). This recipe follows the tradition of the no-cook dried-fruit-and-nut version recorded in 19th-century English confectionery books.",
